TTK29 Project: Hybrid Modeling of Cerebral Hemodynamics

This project investigates how computational models can explain task-related changes in brain activity as measured by fMRI BOLD signals. I focus on the Balloon-Windkessel hemodynamic model, which describes how neuronal activity influences cerebral blood flow, blood volume, and deoxyhemoglobin content, and compare it with a physics-informed neural network (PINN) and a hybrid CoSTA model that combines mechanistic modeling with learned residual corrections. The goal is to evaluate which modeling approach best captures the underlying physiology and reproduces observed BOLD responses.

The project uses openly available task-fMRI data from the ds000030 dataset and compares the models in terms of fitting accuracy, robustness, and interpretability. By integrating classical physiological modeling with machine learning, this work explores the balance between physical realism and predictive flexibility in modeling cerebral hemodynamics. The broader aim is to better understand how neural activity is translated into measurable vascular signals and to assess whether hybrid models can improve the modeling of complex neurovascular dynamics.
